import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 576 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 577 | 2026-10-20 | **Scales: one hand per fingering lesson, and both hands together (her call, 12:32).** The two fingering lessons are now five: 4 `sc-wr` white keys, right hand; 5 `sc-wl` white keys, left hand (each: whole picture, one by one, one strip of groups for every white-key start with the single odd key marked ≠, then \"standard or different?\"); 6 `sc-br` black keys, right hand, which says outright that a right-hand scale starting on a black key always starts on finger 2 and the thumb goes on the first white key that comes up (B♭ C, E♭ F, A♭ C, D♭ F, G♭/F♯ C♭), then tap-the-thumb-keys; 7 `sc-bl` black keys, left hand (one shape 3-2-1, 4-3-2-1, 3 for B♭ E♭ A♭ D♭, plus G♭); 8 `sc-both` both hands together: one column per note, right-hand fingers above, left-hand below, in the group colours, and her turn (the left hand's finger for each note). Octave hook: `scFingers(key, hand, n)` reads `SCALES[key].octaves[n]` and falls back to `rhPattern/lhPattern(n)` for C G D A E, so the both-hands lesson already offers 1, 2 and 4 octaves (4 only for the five standard keys, others greyed); the grid scrolls sideways beyond 8 columns. The single-hand lessons are still one octave. Home p-scale lessons updated. Concept now: 1 pattern, 2 same pattern, 3 degrees, 4-7 fingering, 8 both hands; next 9 all twelve keys, 10 chromatic, 11 the minors. Korean wording is mine, unverified. Built by `patch583.py` + a width fix (`t585`). Checked in English and Korean, no errors. Local commit only, not pushed. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
